WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.240
Hello, and welcome back to another video.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:03.530
with ItsDrDan.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:07.689
And today we're going to be learning
how to read a solubility table.

00:00:08.219 --> 00:00:12.890
Specifically, this one is designed for
students at my school where we use a

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:17.489
very specific solubility sheet, which
anybody that here is allowed, obviously

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:19.140
allowed to use that's watching this.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.200
Um, but we're going to use
that one in specifically.

00:00:22.669 --> 00:00:24.200
So what is it all about?

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.240
So solubility is this concept of
how, when we throw an ionic salt.

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:34.099
into water, it has the
possibility to break apart.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.709
Now it all depends on the strength
of the ionic bond and whether or

00:00:38.709 --> 00:00:43.649
not the water molecules that are
surrounding it are strong enough to

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.519
dissociate and break apart that bond.

00:00:47.099 --> 00:00:49.420
So what is this exactly used for?

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.720
Well, what we're going to be using this
for is a bunch of different skills that

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.039
are really important in the introductory
chemistry and in general chemistry.

00:00:57.495 --> 00:01:01.995
So it's mainly used for determining
solubility, so we're trying to figure

00:01:01.995 --> 00:01:04.524
out what the states of matter are.

00:01:04.905 --> 00:01:09.715
So we want to know is it a solid, is
it a liquid, is it a gas, or can we

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:16.034
actually write that it is aqueous, or
putting an AQ after the chemical species.

00:01:16.455 --> 00:01:19.905
So that's one of the most
important things, is can you

00:01:19.905 --> 00:01:21.535
figure out if that is the thing?

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:27.890
Because that will tell you the net ionic
equation of these different Species.

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:32.370
Now why is that also important is
because that helps illustrate what

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:35.870
the chemistry is that's occurring
within the chemical reaction.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:40.170
Remember, chemical reactions are
all about the chemistry of change.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:45.230
So you need to see like a new molecule
being formed, something being created.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:49.440
And the easiest way for this to be
illustrated is are we making a solid,

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:52.810
a liquid, a gas when this is happening?

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:54.749
That is one of the biggest things.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:55.110
So.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.390
In chemical reactions, it's used a lot.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.020
This also helps illustrate oxidation
and reduction reactions, being

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:05.839
that this will help show you the
charge of cations and anions.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:11.410
And it also helps you figure out this
concept known as electrolytes, meaning

00:02:11.429 --> 00:02:15.950
that most of the ones in this table are
considered to be strong electrolytes.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:20.490
Meaning that they dissolve about
100 percent of the time, whereas

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:24.420
weak is maybe closer to like 5 to
10 percent of the time dissolving.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:28.719
And non electrolytes just means that,
okay, we have this polar substance

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:31.290
surrounded by a polar substance.

00:02:31.949 --> 00:02:36.180
This also helps us get a little bit
of idea of colligative properties,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:40.930
which is essentially how many ions
are in solution can really affect

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.300
how, um, how they behave, uh, when
it comes to their freezing point.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.430
Their boiling point and some
other properties as well.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:50.400
So we're going to dive into this.

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:53.480
Let's take a look and we'll go
through some different examples

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:58.180
of how this particular thing
works in our chemistry course.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.450
So stay tuned and let's take a look.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.310
Our agenda today is to determine
the solubility of a salt.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.120
Now don't forget what is a salt.

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.400
When that is said, well, the
first thing you can think of

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.189
is sodium chloride, right?

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:18.859
What you put on your
food every single day.

00:03:19.345 --> 00:03:25.155
Salts are a cation and an anion bound
together, meaning that you have a

00:03:25.165 --> 00:03:30.994
positive species binding to a negative
species, so ionic characteristics.

00:03:31.515 --> 00:03:35.304
So when we're looking at these, what
you're going to be using is, in our

00:03:35.345 --> 00:03:38.255
CLELC course, we have the green sheet.

00:03:38.284 --> 00:03:42.495
I made a previous video on the
naming component of the green sheet.

00:03:43.015 --> 00:03:45.565
This one is on the other
side, the back half.

00:03:45.875 --> 00:03:47.975
Which is on the solubility side.

00:03:48.405 --> 00:03:51.705
Now what this thing looks like
is essentially you see that

00:03:51.705 --> 00:03:53.985
there are two major columns here.

00:03:54.555 --> 00:04:01.685
The first one tells you, has a list of
cations and anions that are available

00:04:02.304 --> 00:04:04.205
in your whatever compound you have.

00:04:04.765 --> 00:04:07.625
And what this tells you is
the different rule sets.

00:04:08.005 --> 00:04:11.025
So it's separated into two major parts.

00:04:11.435 --> 00:04:15.324
The top part is referred
to as the soluble part.

00:04:16.005 --> 00:04:20.294
So how we read these is, let's
say if I have sodium chloride as

00:04:20.294 --> 00:04:24.585
an example, and I want to know
is, duh, is it soluble in water?

00:04:25.024 --> 00:04:29.455
Well, the way that this works is you
need to break it down into its two

00:04:29.495 --> 00:04:32.584
ionic parts, the cation and the anion.

00:04:33.115 --> 00:04:36.665
So if you can think, well, what is your
cation, remember that was always written

00:04:36.855 --> 00:04:44.455
first, so put Na and then next you're
going to put Cl for the other part.

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:49.130
Now, this is where the solubility chart
comes in handy because what we're trying

00:04:49.130 --> 00:04:52.570
to do is see does this happen or not.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.510
So the solubility chart will verify this.

00:04:56.059 --> 00:04:59.299
So what we're going to look
for is either chlorine or

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:00.580
we're going to look for sodium.

00:05:01.159 --> 00:05:04.470
And what we are going to look at this
first is, alright, if I look at the

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:09.570
very, very first part, I see group one
elements, which is referring to the

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.340
first column of your periodic table.

00:05:12.689 --> 00:05:14.339
We see sodium plus.

00:05:14.825 --> 00:05:19.445
So, what this tells you is we'll
then look at the entire column here.

00:05:20.015 --> 00:05:24.545
And one thing you'll notice up here, it
says that it's usually soluble, okay?

00:05:24.545 --> 00:05:29.624
And it's soluble except when it's
bound to one of these other elements.

00:05:30.235 --> 00:05:33.865
So being that we see sodium is right
here in the middle, so that means

00:05:33.865 --> 00:05:39.685
it's soluble unless it's bound to
essentially, well, okay, it says lithium

00:05:39.685 --> 00:05:43.935
is slightly soluble unless it's bound
to carbonate, phosphate, and fluorine.

00:05:44.409 --> 00:05:45.260
And that's not the case.

00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:47.010
So that means it's soluble.

00:05:47.659 --> 00:05:50.750
There's another way you can look at
it too is via the chlorine side, which

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:52.480
we see, okay, chlorine's right here.

00:05:53.049 --> 00:05:57.320
And what we can do is we see that
chlorine is soluble except when it's

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.530
bound to silver, mercury, and lead.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:05.020
And being that it's bound to sodium, and
it's not one of those three elements,

00:06:05.550 --> 00:06:07.849
meaning once again that it's soluble.

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:15.560
So, what we will do is we will write
an AQ next to sodium chloride, meaning

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:20.139
that it's going to dissolve, and the
way that it dissolves is it's going

00:06:20.139 --> 00:06:23.959
to turn into sodium and chlorine.

00:06:25.029 --> 00:06:26.910
So it's something a little like that.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.700
Let's take a look at another
example using our table.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:35.510
Okay, so our next example, we have silver
chloride at the bottom of the screen.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.880
And what we're going to do once
again is see, is it soluble?

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:41.300
So what we're going to do is
we're going to write both the

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:43.789
cation and the anion components.

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:50.399
Our cation is silver, and then the anion
in this case is going to be chlorine.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:54.020
So what we'll do is we're going
to look once again and see if we

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:58.169
see either of those two elements
to find out, is this soluble?

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:02.599
Now, typically you're looking
for the more common element is

00:07:02.599 --> 00:07:04.159
what's listed on this table.

00:07:04.600 --> 00:07:09.090
So, if you kind of look, you're not going
to see silver anywhere on this list.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:12.200
That's because it's a transition
metal, it's a, it's a precious

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:13.640
metal, you don't usually see it.

00:07:13.930 --> 00:07:15.000
But chlorine, right?

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.469
It's a nion that's like in our,
in our blood system, it's in our

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.250
body, it's in water that we drink.

00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:21.919
It's very common to find in solution.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:27.130
So once again, we see chlorine here
and it says it's soluble except

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.770
for when it is bound to silver.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:30.770
Ooh, okay.

00:07:31.159 --> 00:07:33.100
So we have one, an exception.

00:07:33.555 --> 00:07:38.495
So what does that mean is that chlorine
is always soluble except when it's

00:07:38.495 --> 00:07:41.115
bound to silver, mercury, or lead.

00:07:41.685 --> 00:07:44.034
So, we can't write this then.

00:07:44.565 --> 00:07:48.064
What we are then going to do is,
okay, this is not going to happen.

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:51.214
We can erase this here.

00:07:51.855 --> 00:07:55.995
And instead, what we are going
to end up doing is putting a

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:59.745
S next to our silver chloride.

00:08:00.159 --> 00:08:01.500
And what does that mean?

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:05.229
It stands for that it is a
solid, and in this case it is

00:08:05.229 --> 00:08:08.200
a precipitate in the reaction.

00:08:08.349 --> 00:08:12.140
Okay, so it's going to, you're going
to see this, uh, solid that's going to

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.400
form when you are mixing this together.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.079
Alright, so that's one way to read this.

00:08:18.099 --> 00:08:19.119
Let's look at another.

00:08:19.599 --> 00:08:21.820
Alright, so let's look
at a third example here.

00:08:21.820 --> 00:08:24.260
So we have ammonium hydroxide.

00:08:24.739 --> 00:08:26.710
So how exactly are we going to do that?

00:08:26.710 --> 00:08:28.529
Well, you've got to
remember your diatomics.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:29.270
Right.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:36.320
The first one is NH4 plus, and we are
going to be adding that to OH minus.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:40.389
Now one thing about ammonium is ammonium
is always soluble no matter what,

00:08:40.519 --> 00:08:42.250
but still good to always check this.

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:46.800
So when we're looking at this, we
see ammonium is on the top rule

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:49.800
here, so it is going to be soluble.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:52.650
And it says, except when
bound to, well, it's only when

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:53.940
lithium is bound to something.

00:08:54.655 --> 00:08:57.865
But we can also look at the hydroxide
side to verify this as well.

00:08:58.135 --> 00:09:00.015
It should be the exact
same thing for both.

00:09:00.744 --> 00:09:05.174
Now the one thing is you will notice
is that hydroxide is down here at

00:09:05.344 --> 00:09:10.894
the bottom, and this is where it's
the insoluble area of the table.

00:09:11.034 --> 00:09:15.384
Okay, so usually these ones
on the bottom are insoluble.

00:09:16.014 --> 00:09:22.415
So if this is to agree with what it said
with ammonium, it should be an exception.

00:09:22.965 --> 00:09:28.475
So, what we can look at is, okay, a
hydroxide is insoluble except when

00:09:28.475 --> 00:09:31.995
it's bound to group one and ammonium.

00:09:32.405 --> 00:09:38.135
So what that tells you is that, all right,
well, this is going to be soluble, and

00:09:38.145 --> 00:09:44.005
you can also see it up here, right, except
when bound to ammonium for oxygen as well.

00:09:44.015 --> 00:09:47.535
So you notice how it's almost
everywhere we see ammonium.

00:09:47.995 --> 00:09:49.585
It's a, it's a big exception rule.

00:09:49.585 --> 00:09:51.035
So it's always soluble.

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:57.480
So, what we can do here is that we can
further alter our, our, um, formula,

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:03.690
so we'll put a Q after all these
species to show, hey, this is going to

00:10:03.699 --> 00:10:08.710
be soluble, so when we're writing our
ionic equations, let's do another one.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:12.120
Next one is more just, these next
two, I should say, are just more

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:16.080
to show you some of the basic
rules about the solubility table.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:19.459
Not everything is going
to be soluble, right?

00:10:19.459 --> 00:10:22.300
That's one thing we already saw is
that some things will be solids.

00:10:22.709 --> 00:10:25.220
There are a couple others that
you have to be aware of as well.

00:10:25.539 --> 00:10:27.049
One is your gases.

00:10:27.119 --> 00:10:30.689
So make sure you know
your seven diatomic gases.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:35.509
So if you ever see them, which are, if
you remember, have no fear of ice cold

00:10:35.519 --> 00:10:41.830
beer, which is hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen,
fluorine, bromine, iodine, and chlorine.

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:45.130
So we see hydrogen and oxygen here.

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:50.810
These are gases, so you never write
them as aqueous, they are always gases.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:55.220
Now with water, water is a liquid.

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:57.869
So when do we write L's, right?

00:10:57.869 --> 00:10:59.939
Aqueous is also a solution.

00:11:00.510 --> 00:11:04.489
L is a liquid, which is the same
phase, but there's a difference.

00:11:04.979 --> 00:11:06.969
Liquid means that it's pure.

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:12.780
Aqueous means that it's a solution
made in water, so there is a salt.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:17.600
Dissolved in water, which is what aq is,
but it doesn't really make much sense

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.690
to say water is dissolved in water.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:24.520
We just say it's a liquid, so we
don't use the table for water.

00:11:24.550 --> 00:11:27.150
So, keep that in mind for
yourself, write that down.

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:28.270
It's really important to know.

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:31.890
Same thing with your gases, you got
to make sure you're able to denote

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:35.090
solid, liquid, gas and aqueous.

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:37.460
It's very important to
understand what's happening.

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:40.060
Let's look at another
example, just like this one.

00:11:40.475 --> 00:11:44.115
Okay, so here we have silver
chloride and it's being formed.

00:11:44.425 --> 00:11:46.545
This was the one of the
ones that we did earlier.

00:11:46.585 --> 00:11:47.805
It was our second example.

00:11:48.365 --> 00:11:53.105
So here I have where you have
silver and chlorine are combining

00:11:53.115 --> 00:11:54.925
to make silver chloride.

00:11:55.365 --> 00:12:00.055
So chlorine, as I said earlier,
is a diatomic gas, so we

00:12:00.055 --> 00:12:01.584
gotta leave that labeled as G.

00:12:02.244 --> 00:12:08.165
Another thing is the difference between
the ion and the metal version of it.

00:12:08.645 --> 00:12:14.305
So notice here I have silver and
there's no charge listed next to it.

00:12:14.935 --> 00:12:17.455
This is extremely important to realize.

00:12:17.505 --> 00:12:18.785
There is a difference.

00:12:19.275 --> 00:12:23.735
So if there's no charge and it's
a metal by itself, it means that

00:12:23.825 --> 00:12:28.385
it's the atomic version of it,
meaning it has a charge of zero.

00:12:28.405 --> 00:12:30.035
It's not the ion.

00:12:30.565 --> 00:12:30.965
Okay.

00:12:30.965 --> 00:12:35.185
So this is not equal to a G plus.

00:12:36.765 --> 00:12:37.995
Different from each other.

00:12:37.995 --> 00:12:38.355
Okay.

00:12:38.355 --> 00:12:39.445
So keep that in mind.

00:12:39.455 --> 00:12:40.655
They are not the same.

00:12:41.365 --> 00:12:45.445
So if you see just a metal
sitting there in an equation by

00:12:45.445 --> 00:12:48.495
itself, it is the solid version.

00:12:48.575 --> 00:12:51.655
So you have a solid gas
solid in this equation.

00:12:52.125 --> 00:12:55.605
So you don't always use the
solubility table all the time.

00:12:55.625 --> 00:12:56.685
You have to use judgment.

00:12:57.315 --> 00:13:01.995
This is designed for salt, meaning
it is an ionic equation, right?

00:13:01.995 --> 00:13:02.755
So metal.

00:13:02.755 --> 00:13:02.824
Okay.

00:13:03.115 --> 00:13:06.465
And a nonmetal cation
anion bound together.

00:13:06.935 --> 00:13:11.375
Alright, the last thing with your green
sheet is that there are two different

00:13:11.384 --> 00:13:13.465
pieces that are included with it as well.

00:13:13.865 --> 00:13:18.005
So here I have one of them, the
other is in my gas evolution video.

